WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 264: John D. Rockefeller: The Standard Oil Trust, the Pipeline Monopoly, and the Empire of Kerosene

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.280
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the architectural mastermind of American corporate capitalism.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:23.360
[Rebecca] The austere, deeply religious bookkeeper who organized the chaotic petroleum industry into the world's most formidable monopoly.

5
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of John D.

6
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.360
[Rebecca] Rockefeller.

7
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:39.440
[George] Born in July 1839 in Richford, New York, John Davison Rockefeller was raised under the contradictory influences of two radically different parents.

8
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:53.200
[George] His father, William Avery Devil Bill Rockefeller, was an itinerant confidence man, bigamist, and self-styled botanic physician who sold cancer cures and openly bragged that he cheated his sons to make them sharp.

9
00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:03.280
[George] His mother, Eliza Davison, was a devout, deeply pious Baptist who instilled in John an unbending moral code of thrift, self-discipline, and charity.

10
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:21.440
[George] Moving to Cleveland, Ohio, in 1853, 16-year-old John spent six blistering summer weeks walking the sweltering streets in a black wool suit, knocking on every warehouse and bank door until merchant firm Hewitt and Tuttle hired him as an assistant bookkeeper on September 26.

11
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.680
[George] 1855, a date he celebrated as his job day more reverently than his own birthday.

12
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:40.400
[George] Rockefeller purchased a small red notebook labeled Ledger A, in which he recorded every penny he earned and spent, tithing 10% of his meager weekly earnings to the Baptist Church from his very first paycheck.

13
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.600
[George] Ledger A became his holy scriptures of commerce.

14
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] He developed an obsessive reverence for balance sheets, mathematical precision, and the elimination of waste, forming a Produce commission partnership in 1859 that profited handsomely by supplying food to Union armies during the Civil War.

15
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:08.600
[George] Eliza Rockefeller's strict Calvinist training instilled in young John an absolute terror of debt, teaching him that financial waste was a moral offense against the Almighty.

16
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:22.520
[George] His father's frequent, mysterious disappearances forced young John to shoulder adult financial responsibilities for his mother and younger siblings, reinforcing his profound conviction that stability and order were the highest virtues of life.

17
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:35.240
[George] His meticulous entries in Ledger A documented every cent donated to Sunday school libraries, foreign missions, and impoverished widows, proving that charity was an integral discipline of his earliest youth.

18
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:51.240
[George] In 1859, Edwin Drake struck oil at Titusville in northwestern Pennsylvania, touching off a wild, anarchic petroleum frenzy that turned peaceful valleys into muddy, lawless boomtowns choked with derricks, wooden barrels, and reckless speculators.

19
00:02:51.240 --> 00:03:07.640
[George] While fortune hunters gambled their lives drilling wildcat wells that frequently ran dry, 24-year-old Rockefeller recognized that drilling was pure gambling, while the real, permanent power lay in refining crude petroleum into safe, illuminating kerosene.

20
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:24.200
[George] In 1863, collaborating with chemist Samuel Andrews and partner Morris Clark, Rockefeller constructed a state of the art refinery in Cleveland along the Kingsbury Run Canal, connecting it to both the Cuyahoga River and the Atlantic and Great Western Railroad.

21
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:40.440
[George] Rockefeller abhorred waste with religious fervor, while rival refiners dumped foul smelling byproducts into the river, Rockefeller recovered gasoline for fuel, lubricating oils for industrial machinery, paraffin wax for candles, and petroleum jelly.

22
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:56.000
[George] In 1870, Rockefeller and his associates incorporated the Standard Oil Company with $1 million in capital, adopting the name Standard to promise consumers a guaranteed, uniform grade of kerosene that would not explode in household lamps.

23
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:07.440
[George] The Standard Oil Refinery in Cleveland was designed as an industrial model of cleanliness and efficiency, utilizing steamjacketed pipes and underground tanks to minimize fire hazards.

24
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:18.480
[George] By designing his own fleet of specialized tank cars and acid-resistant tank wagons, Rockefeller seized control over every physical link connecting the oil wellhead to the urban lamp.

25
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:28.720
[George] By establishing his own cooperage to manufacture wooden oak barrels and buying his own timber tracts, Rockefeller slashed the cost of shipping containers by more than half.

26
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:38.240
[George] The early petroleum market was plagued by ruinous volatility, overproduction, and wild price crashes that threatened every refiner with bankruptcy.

27
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:50.000
[George] Rockefeller viewed chaotic competition as a destructive sin, believing that the only salvation lay in bringing the entire industry under a single, rational, harmonious corporate command.

28
00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:07.200
[George] In 1871, Rockefeller partnered with railroad barons in the secretive South Improvement Company, negotiating immense secret rebates on his own oil shipments and drawbacks, a kickback paid by the railroads to Standard Oil on every barrel of oil shipped by his competitors.

29
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] Armed with this lethal logistical advantage, 32-year-old Rockefeller executed the Cleveland Massacre between February and March 1872, summoning 26 independent Cleveland refiners to his office, Rockefeller laid his balance sheets before them, showed them that competition was suicide, and offered to buy them out in Standard oil stock or cash.

30
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:40.600
[George] In less than six weeks, Standard Oil acquired 22 of its 26 competitors, securing control of over a quarter of the nation's total refining capacity overnight.

31
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:50.920
[George] The South Improvement Company scheme provoked armed protests by independent drillers in the oil regions, who marched through Titusville burning effigies of the Cleveland refiners.

32
00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.720
[George] The secret rebates were negotiated with ice-cold mathematical leverage.

33
00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:07.000
[George] Standard Oil guaranteed railroads steady trainloads of hundreds of cars daily, eliminating empty backhauls and transforming chaotic freight traffic into predictable industrial profit.

34
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:18.760
[George] Rockefeller's consolidation of the Cleveland refineries eliminated ruinous overcapacity, creating an integrated manufacturing hub capable of processing tens of thousands of barrels daily.

35
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:41.080
[George] When independent oil producers attempted to bypass Rockefeller's railroad stranglehold by constructing the Tidewater Pipeline, a daring 110-mile underground iron pipe carrying crude over the Allegheny Mountains to Williamsport, Rockefeller recognized that pipelines were the future of oil transportation, deploying immense capital.

36
00:06:41.080 --> 00:07:00.760
[George] Standard Oil constructed a colossal transcontinental network of thousands of miles of subterranean pipelines connecting Pennsylvania oil fields directly to coastal refineries in New York, Philadelphia, Baltimore, and Cleveland, crushing competition and capturing 90% of all oil transport in America.

37
00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:09.080
[George] To unify this sprawling empire across multiple states with restrictive corporate charter laws, Standard Oil attorney Samuel C.T.

38
00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.520
[George] Dodd conceived the trust in 1882.

39
00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:21.040
[George] Thirty-seven stockholders surrendered their shares to nine trustees, headed by Rockefeller, creating the world's first modern monopoly trust.

40
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:34.960
[George] Standard Oil conquered global markets, exporting kerosene in distinctive blue tins across Europe, Russia, China, and Latin America, bringing light to peasant huts that had known only darkness for centuries.

41
00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:46.240
[George] The Standard Oil Pipeline Network functioned as an invisible, subterranean railway, pumping crude petroleum continuously across mountain ranges to deepwater ocean ports.

42
00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:59.920
[George] His global marketing campaign popularized the phrase oil for the lamps of China, distributing 8 million small tin lamps at cost to Asian households to create an insatiable overseas appetite for standard oil kerosene.

43
00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:12.800
[George] The 1882 Trust Agreement established a unified executive committee that met daily at 26 Broadway in Manhattan, analyzing worldwide market reports and coordinating global oil distribution.

44
00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:26.480
[George] By the 1890s, Standard Oil was the most feared and hated corporation on Earth, perceived as an all-powerful, rapacious octopus whose tentacles strangled independent enterprise and corrupted state legislatures.

45
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:30.560
[George] In November 1902, investigative journalist Ida M.

46
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:45.840
[George] Tarbell, whose own father had been ruined as an independent producer during the Cleveland Massacre, began publishing her sensational, meticulously documented 19 part expose in McClure's magazine, The History of the Standard Oil Company.

47
00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:56.400
[George] Tarbell laid bare the secret rebates, espionage networks, price slashing tactics, and predatory dismantling of small businesses that built Rockefeller's empire.

48
00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:04.760
[George] Rockefeller refused to respond publicly, referring to Tarbell dismissively as that poisonous woman and advising associates.

49
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.040
[George] Not a word.

50
00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.680
[George] The world will yet see who is right.

51
00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:19.160
[George] President Theodore Roosevelt made Standard Oil the premier target of his trust busting campaign, while state and federal prosecutors filed antitrust lawsuits across the country.

52
00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:31.000
[George] Ida Tarbell's investigative journalism drew upon public court records, congressional testimony, and insider interviews, setting the benchmark for 20th century muckraking journalism.

53
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:44.600
[George] Ida Tarbell's relentless investigations revealed that even Rockefeller's closest associates feared his silent, inscrutable judgment, describing him as a financial spider sitting at the center of an invisible global web.

54
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:55.720
[George] The public outrage ignited by Tarbell's articles compelled politicians across party lines to demand federal antitrust legislation to curb the unchecked power of industrial trusts.

55
00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:04.440
[George] On May 15, 1911, the United States Supreme Court delivered a historic, unanimous landmark ruling in Standard Oil Co.

56
00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:05.640
[George] of New Jersey v.

57
00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:06.920
[George] United States.

58
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:21.720
[George] Chief Justice Edward Douglas White ruled that Standard Oil had violated the Sherman Antitrust Act through unreasonable restraint of trade and monopolization, ordering the colossal monopoly to be completely dissolved into 34 independent.

59
00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:29.880
[George] Competing companies within six months, including companies that became Exxon, Mobil, Chevron, Amoko, and Conoco.

60
00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:43.400
[George] But the court's decree produced an astonishing, unintended economic paradox, as the nascent automobile industry exploded with the mass production of Henry Ford's Model T, the global demand for gasoline skyrocketed.

61
00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:49.600
[George] The individual shares of the 34 severed daughter companies surged astronomically on Wall Street.

62
00:10:49.600 --> 00:11:09.600
[George] Because Rockefeller retained massive equity stakes in all 34 successor corporations, his personal fortune nearly tripled, reaching an estimated $900 million, making him the world's first verified billionaire, possessing approximately 2% of the entire gross domestic product of the United States.

63
00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:21.680
[George] The Supreme Court's 1911 dissolution decree established the rule of reason in antitrust jurisprudence, distinguishing between legitimate commercial expansion and predatory monopolization.

64
00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:34.560
[George] The court's mandate forced the birth of modern corporate compliance, setting the legal boundaries that defined the relationship between sovereign democratic states and colossal multinational corporations for the next century.

65
00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:44.480
[George] The surging popularity of gasoline-powered automobiles transformed the former waste product of petroleum refining into the most valuable commercial commodity on Earth.

66
00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:55.440
[George] Having retired from active corporate management in the late 1890s, Rockefeller applied the same rigorous, scientific principles of efficiency and organization to philanthropy.

67
00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.640
[George] Assisted by his brilliant Baptist advisor Frederick T.

68
00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.120
[George] Gates and his devoted son John D.

69
00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:02.960
[George] Rockefeller J.R.

70
00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.960
[George] Rockefeller created the modern philanthropic foundation.

71
00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:14.800
[George] He founded the University of Chicago, transforming a struggling Baptist college into a world class research institution.

72
00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:33.160
[George] Established the Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research, now Rockefeller University, which conquered yellow fever, discovered antibiotics, and revolutionized modern immunology, and created the Rockefeller Sanitary Commission, which eradicated hookworm disease across the American South.

73
00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:43.400
[George] In his daily life, the frail, bald titan walked his Caicuit estate in Picantico Hills, New York, giving shiny new dimes to children and smiling quietly.

74
00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:44.680
[George] When John D.

75
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:53.000
[George] Rockefeller died in May 1937 at age 97, he had given away over $140 million.

76
00:12:53.000 --> 00:13:08.280
[George] His legacy remains as complex as the industrial age he created, the ruthless corporate conqueror who monopolized an empire, and the scientific benefactor whose foundations permanently elevated modern medicine and saved millions of human lives.

77
00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.480
[George] Frederick T.

78
00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:20.600
[George] Gates' vision of scientific philanthropy transformed medical education in the United States, implementing the landmark Flexner report that modernized American medical schools.

79
00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:32.680
[George] Rockefeller's historic gifts to medical research laid the scientific foundation for modern global health, demonstrating that industrial fortunes could be channeled into the permanent eradication of human disease.

80
00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:44.280
[George] Rockefeller's enduring legacy is written in the eradication of infectious diseases, the founding of modern biomedical science, and the enduring architecture of modern global commerce.

81
00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:55.320
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non president American series continues with episode 265.

82
00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:04.040
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

